/*
------------------------------------------------------------
Problem    : Reverse Linked List II
Platform   : LeetCode
Topic      : Linked List
Difficulty : Medium

Problem:
Given the head of a singly linked list and two positions
left and right, reverse the nodes between positions left
and right.

The nodes outside this range must remain unchanged.

Example:

    Input:
        head = [1,2,3,4,5]
        left = 2
        right = 4

    Output:
        [1,4,3,2,5]

Approach:
Use a dummy node and reverse only the required portion of
the linked list.

A dummy node is useful because the reversal may begin at
position 1, in which case there is no actual node before the
reversed section.

We use:

    prev
        Points to the node immediately before the section
        that needs to be reversed.

    curr
        Points to the first node of the section.

    prevNode
        Used to reverse the links inside the section.

    last
        Stores the original first node of the section.
        After reversal, this becomes the last node of the
        reversed section.

Step-by-step:

    head = [1,2,3,4,5]
    left = 2
    right = 4

First move prev to the node before position left:

    dummy -> 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5
             ^
            prev

Now:

    curr = 2
    last = 2

Reverse the section [2,3,4]:

Before:

    1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 -> 5

After reversing:

    1 -> 4 -> 3 -> 2    5

Finally reconnect the reversed section:

    prev->next = prevNode

and connect the original first node of the section
(now the last node) to the remaining list:

    last->next = curr

Final:

    1 -> 4 -> 3 -> 2 -> 5

Why do we need a dummy node?

Consider:

    head = [1,2,3]
    left = 1
    right = 2

The reversal starts from the head.

Without a dummy node, there is no node before the reversed
section to reconnect the list.

The dummy node gives us:

    dummy -> 1 -> 2 -> 3

which makes the same logic work even when left = 1.

Algorithm:
1. Create a dummy node before head.
2. Move `prev` to the node immediately before `left`.
3. Store the first node of the section in `last`.
4. Reverse exactly `right - left + 1` nodes.
5. Connect `prev` to the new first node of the reversed section.
6. Connect `last` to the node after the reversed section.
7. Return `dummy->next`.

Time Complexity : O(n)

Reason:
We traverse the list to reach `left` and reverse the
required portion once.

Space Complexity : O(1)

Reason:
Only a constant number of pointers are used.

------------------------------------------------------------
*/

class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* reverseBetween(ListNode* head, int left, int right) {

        // No reversal needed
        if (!head || left == right) {
            return head;
        }

        // Dummy node handles the case when left == 1
        ListNode* dummy = new ListNode(0);
        dummy->next = head;

        // Move prev to the node before position left
        ListNode* prev = dummy;

        for (int i = 0; i < left - 1; i++) {
            prev = prev->next;
        }

        // First node of the section being reversed
        ListNode* last = prev->next;

        ListNode* curr = last;
        ListNode* prevNode = nullptr;

        // Reverse nodes from left to right
        for (int i = 0; i < right - left + 1; i++) {

            ListNode* nextNode = curr->next;

            curr->next = prevNode;

            prevNode = curr;
            curr = nextNode;
        }

        // Connect the node before the reversed section
        // to the new first node of the reversed section
        prev->next = prevNode;

        // Connect the original first node (now the last node)
        // to the remaining part of the list
        last->next = curr;

        return dummy->next;
    }
};
